  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\n\nThe central idea is to minimize the maximum shift overlap of a step function h(x) on [0,2] with integral 1 using gradient descent and grid refinement. The key bottleneck is the non-convexity of the overlap integral and discrete nature of the optimization. Starting with the given initial solution (n=45), we:\n\n- Use gradient descent with analytical derivatives to optimize within each grid\n- Double the grid resolution (n_points) up to 360 to capture finer details\n- Maintain a history of the best solution from each grid for comparison\n- Use projection to enforce integral and bound constraints during optimization\n\nThis differs from previous strategies by:\n- Using gradient-based optimization with automatic differentiation\n- Retaining parent solution via interpolation rather than histogram matching\n- Maintaining exact steps for grid transfer\n- Allowing variable grid sizes while keeping track across different resolutions\n\nImportant limitations include:\n- Grid doubling introduces new discretization errors\n- Local optima might persist if gradient vanishes\n- Memory usage grows with grid size increases\n\n2. Mathematical specification\n\nVariables:\n- h(x) \u2208 [0,1] for x \u2208 [0,2]\n- n_points: number of samples (15, 30, 60, 120, 240, 480; or start from 45)\n- dx = 2 / n_points\n\nObjective:\n- C5(n) = (max_lag |correlation(h, 1-h)|) * dx\n- Goal: minimize C5(n)\n\nConstraints:\n1. Integral constraint: \n   integral(h) = 1 \u2192 sum(h) = n_points / 2\n2. Box constraints: h[i] \u2208 [0,1] for all i\n\nSpecification details:\n- dx serves as integration weight\n- Mode='full' in np.correlate includes all valid shifts\n- Gradient efficiently captures correlation changes\n- Constraints enforced via clipping + projection\n- Projection projects h to satisfying bounds/sum\n\n3. Implementation procedure\n\nParent validation:\n- Check initial_h_values satisfy integral=45/2 and bounds\n- Compute initial C5_bound to set upper bound\n\nGrid levels:\n0. Base case (n=45): given parent solution\n1. Then n=90, 180, ..., up to defined maximum grid size\n2. For each grid level:\n   a. Interpolate parent from previous level (if available)\n   b. Add dx-dependent scale factor for bounds\n   c. Project to satisfy constraints:\n        h_proj = np.clip(np.pad(parent, pad) or parent + small noise\n        scale factor = (n_points/2) / sum(h_proj)   (projection preserves bounds then integral)\n   d. Run optimization with:\n        Objective: C5_bound(h)\n        Box constraints: [0,1] for all i\n        Integral constraint: enforced via automatic gradient clipping\n\nConstraint handling:\n- Process both bounds and integral during optimization\n- Projection ensures feasible starting points for each grid\n- Automatic differentiation computes gradient for correlation\n\n4. Search choices and variations\n\nThese variations ensure robust exploration:\n\n- Grid sizes: start from given 45 if available, then double up\n- Optimizer method: gradient descent, or L-BFGS-B\n- Step sizes: adaptive by grid: dx * [0.1, 0.5, 1.0] for bound exploration\n- Projection mode: \"clip\" (bound enforcement) or \"scale\" (integral adjustment)\n\nFallback:\n- When gradient is zero, try perturbing h by:\n    h_sample = h @ (X, where X are solution matrices)\n- Maintains feasibility and allows escape from local minima\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\n\nTiming model:\n- O(n^2) per C5 evaluation\n- Grid time doubles with each doubling of n_points\n- Budget monitor checks time at grid start\n- Opt-in higher grid monitoring if significant quality improvement\n\nResilience measures:\n- Exponential backoff for grid size increases\n- Hand-coded gradient processing avoids numerical issues\n- Grid failures trigger fallback to linear-space search\n- Gradient scaling maintains separation near boundaries\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n\nFeasibility checks:\n1. 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 for all i\n2. abs(sum(h) - n_points/2) < 1e-6\n3. Max_C5 is finite\n\nValidation steps:\n1. Re-compute C5_bound from solution candidate\n2. Uniqueness checks for proposed upper bound\n3. Lower-bounds tracking to ensure improvement\n4. Regular fitness updates to reflect dynamic parent performance\n\nReturn value:\n- (h_values, c5_bound, n_points): tuple of (valid solution, improved bound, chosen grid size)\n\nThe solution must pass all validity and performance checks before being considered a valid pass.",
